    apply: "Identify someone in the church whose contribution is often unnoticed and thank them in person or in writing this week. Visit or contact one member who is suffering and offer practical help. When you notice envy or pride in yourself, confess it and give thanks for the gifts of others.",
    activity: "Ask learners to stand in a circle, each representing a body part, and name a task that only their part can do. Then remove a few and discuss what is lost. Read 1 Corinthians 12:12-26 and discuss how the church functions when everyone participates.",
    takeHome: "Read 1 Corinthians 12:12-31, Romans 12:3-8 and Ephesians 4:11-16. List five unseen contributions in your church and thank the people involved. Memorise 1 Corinthians 12:26.",
    prayer: "Thank God for placing us in his body. Ask forgiveness for envy and pride. Pray for those who suffer and those who are overlooked.",
    teacher: "Some learners feel unnoticed or may have been hurt by church life. Be gentle and affirm each learner's place. Avoid examples that single out particular people. Remember that the aim is to value each member, not to flatter.",
    assess: "Weekly check: ask learners what the body picture teaches about gifts and unity.",
    mcq1: ["What does 1 Corinthians 12:26 say about the body?", ["If one member suffers, all suffer together", "Each member lives alone", "Only leaders suffer", "Suffering is useless"], 0, "Members share in suffering and honour."],
    mcq2: ["How does Paul describe the members that seem weaker?", ["Indispensable", "Unimportant", "Optional", "Temporary"], 0, "They are indispensable."],
    tf: ["Paul teaches that only members with visible gifts are important to the body.", false, "He teaches that every member is needed, including the weaker ones."],
    sa: ["What does the body picture teach about the church and its gifts?", ["body", "members", "needed", "unity", "envy", "honour"], "A good answer says every member and gift is necessary, God arranged them as he chose, envy and pride divide, and members share joys and sorrows."],
